## Frequently Asked Questions

## What qualifies for California lemon law?

Any vehicle sold with a manufacturer’s warranty may be eligible under California lemon law. Most warranties cover the vehicle for 3 years or 36,000 miles from the date of purchase (though [warranty terms](https://www.kbb.com/car-advice/car-warranty-guide/) vary by brand). If a defect is reported to the manufacturer during the warranty period and they fail to fix it after a reasonable number of attempts, you may have a valid claim.

The 18-month / 18,000-mile window is a lemon-law presumption, not a requirement. It does not decide whether you have a good case. What matters is whether the defect first occurred during the original manufacturer warranty. This is only a legal presumption that strengthens your case - it’s not a requirement. As long as the defect arises and is reported within the warranty period, you may still be eligible.

## Can I file a lemon law claim without a Huntington Beach Lemon Law attorney?

Technically, yes. But in practice, it’s challenging. Most manufacturers will delay, undercut, or deny valid claims submitted by individuals without legal representation. An experienced California lemon law attorney knows how to navigate the system, push back against these tactics, and fight for the full compensation you deserve.

## What is the time limit to file a California lemon law claim?

California has a four-year statute of limitations to file a lemon law claim. We strongly recommend taking action as soon as possible. Waiting to file could suggest the defect isn’t serious - or worse, give the manufacturer an excuse to avoid liability.

More importantly, continuing to drive a defective vehicle puts your safety at risk.

## What if I want a replacement vehicle instead of a buyback?

If you prefer to stay with the same brand, you can request a replacement vehicle. This would be the same model or one of similar value. However, manufacturers are only legally obligated to offer a buyback - not a replacement. If they agree to the replacement, it’s usually part of a negotiated settlement.

In most cases, we recommend pursuing a buyback for a lemon vehicle.

## Does California lemon law apply to private car sales?

No. California lemon law only applies to vehicles sold with a warranty, which private sellers are not required to provide. Most private sales are made “as is,” meaning the buyer assumes responsibility for any defects that may be present. If you purchased a car from a private seller and it turns out to be defective, you’ll likely have no legal recourse under lemon law.

## How long does a California lemon law case take to resolve?

Lemon law cases typically settle within a few weeks to a few months - but this varies. At CLINE APC, we strive to resolve claims efficiently, often within 30 days. However, factors like the complexity of your case, the manufacturer’s cooperation, and the availability of documentation can all affect the timeline.
